1. THREE MEASUREMENTS THAT FIX A TRIANGLE: FIGURE 18
   Each set of three is fed to the construction; one triangle comes out.
     (a) ASA: angles 80 and 40, side 10 between them
         sides  11.37   7.42  10.00   angles  80.00  40.00  60.00
     (b) SSS: sides 15, 20, 8
         sides  15.00  20.00   8.00   angles  41.68 117.55  20.77
     (c) SAS: sides 7 and 8, angle 40 between them
         sides   8.00   7.00   5.22   angles  80.38  59.62  40.00
   The other three measurements are forced. Two triangles with the same
   three data are therefore the same triangle in two places: congruent.

2. THREE ANGLES DO NOT FIX A TRIANGLE: PROBLEM 5
   Angles 30, 50, 100 (Figure 17), with the side between 30 and 50 set to 1, 2, 5:
         sides   0.51   0.78   1.00   angles  30.00  50.00 100.00
         sides   1.02   1.56   2.00   angles  30.00  50.00 100.00
         sides   2.54   3.89   5.00   angles  30.00  50.00 100.00
   Same angles, sides in the ratio 1 : 2 : 5. The angles fix the shape and
   nothing else, so Angle-Angle-Angle is the answer to problem 5: it is not a
   case for congruence. It is the first case for similarity.
   And the third angle is never news: it is 180 minus the other two, so AAA
   is really only two measurements.

3. TWO SIDES AND AN ANGLE NOT BETWEEN THEM: THE CASE THAT IS MISSING
   Side a = 8 opposite a 40 degree angle A, and side b = 10:
         sides   8.00  10.00   2.90   angles  40.00 126.54  13.46
         sides   8.00  10.00  12.42   angles  40.00  53.46  86.54
   Two different triangles fit the same three data: side a can swing to
   meet the base in two places. So Side-Side-Angle is not a congruence case,
   unless the angle is 90 degrees, where the swing has one place to land:
         sides  13.00   5.00  12.00   angles  90.00  22.62  67.38
   That special case is Pythagoras: two sides of a right triangle fix the third.
   With a = 5 too short to reach, no triangle fits at all:
         0 triangles

4. SIMILAR MEANS ONE RATIO FOR EVERY PAIR OF SIDES: FIGURES 19 AND 20
     Figure 19  d/a, e/b, f/c       ratios 2, 2, 2
     Figure 20b 10/30, 5/15, 6/18   ratios 1/3, 1/3, 1/3
     Figure 20c 4/6, 12/18 (SAS)    ratios 2/3, 2/3
   Every ratio is the same number, the scale factor. Lengths multiply by it.

5. EXAMPLE 5 AND PROBLEMS 43-46: FIND x AND THE ANGLES
   Match the sides by the angles they sit between, then one ratio gives x.
             known pair  scale k  matches x  x = k * side   A, B, C
     Ex 5        3 -> 5      5/3          6            10   90, 60, 30
     43          4 -> 8        2          2             4   90, 60, 30
     44         12 -> 6      1/2         16             8   30, 75, 75
     45        20 -> 30      3/2         45  135/2 = 67.5   60, 95, 25
     46         10 -> 8      4/5         50            40   50, 125, 5
   The angles are copied, not computed: similar triangles have equal angles.
   Only a length needs the scale factor.

6. TRUE OR FALSE: PROBLEMS 10-12
     10  sides 30, 29, 10 and 30, 29, 10: all three equal, SSS: congruent. True.
     11  angles 25 and 100 in both: two equal angles, AA: similar. True.
     12  120 between 3 and 2, and between 4 and 3: ratios 4/3 = 1.3333
         and 3/2 = 1.5000 differ, so SAS fails: not similar. False.
         Try the other pairing, 4/2 and 3/3: 2 and 1, worse.

7. A SCALE FACTOR k MULTIPLIES AREA BY k^2
     legs 3, 4 scaled by   2: area 6 ->  24, ratio 4
     legs 3, 4 scaled by   3: area 6 ->  54, ratio 9
     legs 3, 4 scaled by 1/2: area 6 -> 3/2, ratio 1/4
   Similar triangles with sides in the ratio 1 : 3 have areas in the ratio 1 : 9.
